---
const base = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')}/`;
const href = (route: string) => `${base}${route.replace(/^\//, '')}`;

export const sectionLinks = [
  { id: 'direct-lattice', label: 'Translations define the direct lattice' },
  { id: 'dual-lattice', label: 'Reciprocal vectors preserve every translation phase' },
  { id: 'distinct-labels', label: 'One Brillouin zone contains distinct k labels' },
  { id: 'path-slice', label: 'A band path is a visualization slice' },
];
export const synthesis = 'Appendix D derives reciprocal space from the phase structure of direct-lattice translations. The dual relation makes every reciprocal vector G invisible to lattice phases, so k and k+G carry equivalent translation labels and one Brillouin zone is sufficient. A high-symmetry line plot samples selected paths through that zone; it neither displays nor integrates the full reciprocal-space volume.';
---

<section aria-labelledby="direct-lattice">
  <h2 id="direct-lattice">Translations define the direct lattice</h2>
  <p>
    A primitive cell becomes an infinite crystal by translations formed from integer combinations of primitive vectors. The cell contains the atomic basis; the lattice is the set of translations. Figure D.1 uses graphene to keep those objects separate: the honeycomb structure requires two carbon atoms per primitive cell even though its Bravais lattice contains one translation point per cell.
  </p>
  <p>
    This geometry matters because periodic electronic and vibrational states are classified by the phase acquired under those translations. Reciprocal space is introduced to organize that phase, not as a numerical afterthought.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="dual-lattice">
  <h2 id="dual-lattice">Reciprocal vectors preserve every translation phase</h2>
  <p>
    The reciprocal primitive vectors are chosen to be dual to the direct primitive vectors. Their defining relation and its phase consequence are
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="direct reciprocal duality and reciprocal translation phase">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>·</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.9em"/><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;a&#125;_i\!\cdot\!\mathbf&#123;b&#125;_j=2\pi\delta_&#123;ij&#125;,\qquad \mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\mathbf&#123;G&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;=1</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The Kronecker delta says that a primitive translation pairs to 2π only with its dual reciprocal primitive vector and to zero with the others. Integer combinations then make the exponential equal to one for every translation R paired with every reciprocal vector G. Adding G to a Bloch label therefore changes none of the phases generated by lattice translations.
  </p>
  <p class="paper-meta">Source locator: Giustino, Appendix D, Eqs. D.1–D.5 and Fig. D.1. Bloch-state interpretation: <a href={href('/core/part-iii/')}>Core III · Periodic Matter and Electronic States</a>.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="distinct-labels">
  <h2 id="distinct-labels">One Brillouin zone contains distinct k labels</h2>
  <p>
    A reciprocal-lattice vector G identifies a phase redundancy; a Bloch wavevector k labels a translation-symmetry sector. They occupy the same reciprocal space but play different roles. Because k and k+G have the same lattice-translation character, one primitive reciprocal cell contains all distinct labels.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first Brillouin zone chooses the Wigner–Seitz cell around the reciprocal origin. Its symmetry makes it a convenient nonredundant region, but convenience is not the reason redundancy disappears: that conclusion already followed from the translation phases. Electronic k, phonon q, and reciprocal-lattice G must remain distinct even when each is restricted or shifted using the same reciprocal geometry.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="path-slice">
  <h2 id="path-slice">A band path is a visualization slice</h2>
  <p>
    Figure D.2 first shows band energies as surfaces over the two-dimensional Brillouin zone, then reduces them to a line plot along Γ–K–M–Γ. The line makes crossings and dispersion easy to see because it discards most k points. That same discard is why it cannot prove the absence of an off-path extremum or Fermi pocket.
  </p>
  <p>
    A Brillouin-zone integral asks a different question and requires weighted sampling of the full zone, reduced by symmetry where justified. The selected high-symmetry path is a visualization and diagnostic slice; the integration mesh is a quadrature rule. A single list of “k points” should never hide this difference.
  </p>
</section>
